{
  "rules": [
    "If sentiment is positive (≥0.2) in recent history, model tends to overestimate prices; reduce forecast slope or add downward bias.",
    "If model shows steady upward trend with no recent dip, true outcome often reverses or drops sharply — apply mean reversion correction after 3–5 consecutive rising days.",
    "After a sharp price spike (e.g., >10% from prior peak), model often overshoots; cap forecast at 1.5x recent peak or use exponential decay to dampen momentum.",
    "When sentiment is neutral or negative (≤0.1), model frequently underestimates downside risk; add 2–5% buffer below forecast for early warning periods.",
    "If true outcome deviates >10% from forecast in first 5 days, adjust future forecast by 10–20% downward for the next 7–10 days to account for lagged market reaction.",
    "Model often fails to capture sentiment-driven volatility; if sentiment spikes, forecast should include 1–3% volatility buffer or adjust mean by ±1.5–2.0 units based on sentiment magnitude."
  ]
}
